WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.659
If you've been learning a language for
some time do you happen to ask yourself

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:09.300
questions such as what level am I at? Am
I actually making progress? When am I

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.219
gonna reach the next level? Am i
improving my grammar, pronunciation or

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:19.650
fluency in that target language? Hi, my
name is Luca from LucaLampariello.com, and

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.850
today we're going to talk about how to
measure progress we're learning a

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:31.980
foreign language. There is really nothing
wrong with asking yourself where you

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.110
stand and where you're going. I did that
all the time. Back in the day, when I was

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.550
at school, when I was fifteen, I can
remember as clear as day that I was

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.739
jotting down all my language goals and I
was fantasizing about learning tons of

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:51.899
languages, and I was writing down the
level at which I thought I was and where

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:57.329
I wanted to go. With time and experience,
I've just realized that while setting up

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:01.800
goals is important, the most important
and efficient way to tackle how to

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.150
measure foreign languages is actually to
set up a system which will allow you to

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:12.439
learn in the long term and that is
developing of the right habits.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.060
How do I build the system? The people who
follow my blog have noticed that I've

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.180
recently published some logbooks where
I keep meticulously track of

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.550
my learning process. I think that having
a logbook is a great idea: you can keep

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.440
track of your progress but how do you
measure progress? Well, one thing that

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.880
I've recently added - and I think it's
making a difference - is actually to add

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.460
some self-reflection exercises. After
writing what I did and how long it took

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.770
me I normally spend two or three minutes
- even 10 minutes when it's necessary

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.190
jotting down the things I did and asking
myself whether the things I'm doing

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:02.229
are actually good for me and how I can
refine them. In fact, the most important

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:07.030
goal is not only to keep track of my
progress but actually to refine the

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.950
system through which I am actually
learning, so I ask things such as "am I

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.170
using the right resource?" "Is this
resource nice and good and

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:21.310
comprehensible and intelligible and
interesting for me at this level? What

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.959
else can I add? How can I organize my
time? And these bits and pieces of

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:30.190
information are actually adding up and
they're helping me refine the system

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:35.650
better and better. Refining the system
means to be able to learn in the long

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:40.209
term - language learning is always and
always will be a marathon and if you

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:44.680
want to be successful, the most important
thing you want to do is actually stick

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.150
to it over the long haul and with time
I've developed a system which allows me

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:55.920
to learn not only one language but two
or even three at the same time.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.099
in language learning there are things you
can more or less measure, for example the

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:10.510
amount of words that you know you can
save them you can look back at them and

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:14.640
say okay this is more or less the list
of words that I know but unfortunately

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.140
language learning is not just a corpus
of knowledge, it's also a skill that you

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:25.180
develop, and it entails a number of subskills in and of itself and within

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.870
itself for example being able to speak
being able to write being able to read

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:34.359
and so on and so forth, and unfortunately
it is much more difficult to not only to

00:03:34.359 --> 00:03:38.290
keep track of this but also to measure
whether you're actually making progress

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:43.810
or not. In his popular book "Peak - secrets
from the new science of expertise", the

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.720
author Anders Ericsson makes the case
that experts in any field actually

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.560
operate within a consistent framework
that they built and this framework is

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.150
based upon consistent mental
representations that these experts

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.409
develop with time it's a really really
interesting book and I recommend you

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:07.180
read it. So how do I operate within the
language learning framework? What are my

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.130
mental representations? I divide language
learning mainly in three areas: beginner

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:18.010
intermediate and advanced. Actually, I
define my level according to the

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:21.430
resources that I use - the what and how I
use them -

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:26.260
the how. Let me give you an example: if
I'm at the very early stage of language

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.669
learning that is I'm a beginner I always
use a book like ASSIMIL or Teach

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:35.470
yourself or another resource and I apply
my bidirectional method to it so I

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:41.169
define the what I know the how and for
me I am at this beginner stage. So after

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:45.220
I finish ASSIMIL, I do not ask myself
whether I have reached an intermediate

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.300
level or not - for me it's actually
automatic - and once I get into the

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.710
intermediate realm, intermediate ground
whatever you want to call it, I just

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:58.960
changed the what - that is the resource or
resources - and I also change the how.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.620
A lot of people think that my
bi-directional translation is a complete

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.419
method to learn a language but the
reality is that mine is a combination of

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:12.669
methods which evolve with time. So, what
do I do when I have reached an

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:15.949
intermediate level? Well, again, I changed the what and I change

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:21.289
the how - most often than not I find some
resources on the internet, podcasts with

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:26.120
scripts and audio that I can keep
working on but I do not use my

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.560
bi-directional translation anymore I do
more intensive listening and reading and

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.189
jotting down vocabulary and also I start
talking with some tutors online or with

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:42.259
some friends and in the advanced phase I
do something different such as starting

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:47.270
watching movies or starting absorbing
more authentic content. The key point

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:53.000
here is that you should not ask yourself
whether you are beginner, intermediate or

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.139
advanced, I think that it's much more
important to focus on building a

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:01.759
consistent system that works for you a
kind of mental framework where you

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:06.080
constantly know where you are and if you
do that for one language then you can

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.430
simply replicate it for any other
language, it really helps you not only

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:14.900
figuring out where you are but also
motivates you in the long run because

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.310
you know where you're going.

00:06:24.219 --> 00:06:28.490
Learning a language is a little bit like
climbing a mountain or setting upon a

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.990
very long journey it takes time it's an
endless journey actually because we

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.379
never stop learning so instead of
focusing whether you're constantly

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:39.830
making progress because if you're
walking or you're climbing or you're

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.009
running you are getting to your
destination, you  are getting closer and closer

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:48.949
I think you should focus on the small
victories that are along the journey. Let

00:06:48.949 --> 00:06:54.409
me give you an example about what I do
when I happen to have these "Eureka"

00:06:54.409 --> 00:06:59.960
moments or celebrate victories. When it
comes to reading I really get excited

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.099
when for example I stumble upon a
Wikipedia article about something I

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:09.620
really like and I understand the gist of
it well that's great! Or when I listen to

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.089
something podcasts or a movie and I
start understanding bits and pieces of

00:07:14.089 --> 00:07:18.229
this I clearly remember when that
happened when I was watching a Swedish

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.909
movie I got so excited about that!
Speaking - when I first have a

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:26.740
conversation on skype with someone and I
can get my point across

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.850
or when a stumble upon someone I am in a
given country and I happen to have a

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:35.050
conversation with someone that happened
in Hungary in Budapest some three years

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.160
ago I started learning Hungarian for
some time and then I started talking to

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.940
this granny who could actually
understand me I was so excited about

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.020
that these are small victories, these are
the things you want to focus on because

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:51.310
these small victories will push you
through, exactly like they will push

00:07:51.310 --> 00:08:00.970
climbers to move from one base camp to
the other you reach this thing. So there

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:06.280
you have it, first: focus on the system
instead of goals. Setting up goals is

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:10.540
fine, but I think that refining the
system is so much better because at the

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.220
end of the day that's what is going to
allow you to learn in the long term

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:18.880
Second: develop your own mental
representations of where you stand and

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.410
where you're going. In my case its
beginner, intermediate, advanced, and each

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:27.310
stage corresponds to a certain kind of
material I use and how I use it

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:33.100
and third: celebrate small victories
because small victories do count and add

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:37.690
up in the long run. As usual, this video
comes with a very lengthy and thorough

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:41.920
article I've put together on my blog. If
you liked the content of this video you

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.180
can click on the link in the description
box and you will find a ton of other

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:51.550
material, resources links and whatnot.
Thank you very much for listening to me

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.410
and I'll talk to you very very soon

